    "media:imagetext": "Section 2\nWednesday PM, October 27,1937.\nDear Family:\nIt's 9 PM. Mr.Bond and I have just boarded the good ship\nKung Wo at Hankow.\nWe each have a large spacious cabin and we are\nboth turning in before the ship sails.\ntrying all day to make our Chinese associates understand a business\nproposal which would be quite easy for a twelve year old child to\nmater in half an hour? Such work 1s what irks one in China.\nto whom we were talking represent the type with whom we must do business.\nare completely worn out.\nThursday.\nOctober 2sth.\nHave just been ashore at KinKlang to look see\nChina shops.\nBought two small porcelain pictures just to have purchased\nsomething where the best modern porcelain is made.\nAll day we have\ntravelled along the broad Yangtze, stopping only at Anking about six\no'clock PM.\nThere we anchored and a huge sampan, alive with Chinese,\ncame alongside,\ntied up to our boat while amidst a scene of utmost chaos\na huge amount of practically worthless cargo came on board, including\nabout 100 coolies.\nAn approximately similar number got off with an\ninferno of walla walla and confusion of small baskets, bundles and boxes.\nFour young wounded soldiers were interesting.\nThey were Nanking boys\nwounded in the fighting in the north and were being sent back to their\nfamilies in Nanking.\nNone of them looked to be over sixteen years old.\nThere are only four first class passengers on this large river boat -\nthe other two are Chinese, one bound for Wuhu where we land early tomorrow\nmorning, and the other for Chinkiang, a town just upstream from the boom\nwhich the Chinese have stretched across the Yangtze to keep the Jap fleet\nout of the river.\nOur Captain and his first mate - both Britishers -\nsay the boom is an effective bar to navigation.\nIt is composed of old\nhulks filled with stones and sunk side by side clear across the river.\nBelow the boom an extensive system of mines makes it dangerous to even\napproach the boom.\nThe small Chinese boats can get around the boom\nthrough a series of canals and are busy carrying Shanghai cargo around\nthe boom,\nwhich cargo is delivered then to English boats which carry it\nin either direction to or away from Shanghai.\nriday, October 29th.\nWent to bed at 9 PM and didn't wake up until 8 AM\nsee I'm not suffering from insomnia.\nThis morning there is a cold\nwind blowing from the north.\nhave just\nleft Wuhu and are due at\nNanking about noon.\nThe weather is bad - which is good - because the\nJaps can't bomb with a low ceiling.\nIf they fly low the anti-airerait\nguns shoot them down and if they fly high they can't see Nanking because\nthere is a solid overcoat at about 3000 feet.\nAlso since International\npressure has been exerted against indiscriminate bombing, that type of\nattack has been practiced only against cities and towns where there are\nno influential foreigners.\n-4-\n68801 SINOS",
